      "problem": "$4\\times 2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{2}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(2\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{12}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{12}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 12}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{48}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{5}=9\\,\\text{R}3=9\\,\\frac{3}{5}\n$$",
